      <span class="blog-cat-tag" style="background:#15803d;">CENTRAL HEATING</span>
      <h1 class="blog-title">Installing Central Heating in a Liverpool Victorian Terrace: What You Need to Know</h1>
      <p style="font-size:1.05rem;color:var(--muted);line-height:1.6;margin-bottom:1.5rem;">Solid walls, wooden floors, high ceilings and no cavity. Fitting central heating in a Liverpool Victorian terrace is a different job from a modern house. Here is what you need to know before you start.</p>
      <div class="blog-meta">
        <span><strong>By</strong> Liverpool Emergency Plumber</span>
        <span><strong>Gas Safe Registered Plumber</strong></span>
        <span>Published April 2026</span>
        <span>Updated 26 November 2026</span>
        <span>6 min read</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section style="background:var(--white);padding:2rem 0 3rem;">
    <div class="blog-post">
      <div class="blog-body">
        <h2>Why Victorian Liverpool Properties Are Different</h2>
        <p>Liverpool has a lot of Victorian and Edwardian terraced houses. You find them in Aigburth, Allerton, Anfield, Toxteth, Sefton Park, Mossley Hill, Wavertree and beyond. They were built before central heating was normal. Some still have none. Others have old systems, added years ago, that do not work well.</p>
        <p>Fitting central heating in a Victorian terrace is not the same as in a 1980s semi. The walls, floors, old pipes and layout all bring their own problems. A good installer knows how to deal with them.</p>
        <CallStrip inline title="Planning new heating?" text="Free survey and a fixed written price before we start." />

        <h2>The Main Challenges in Victorian Liverpool Properties</h2>

        <h3>Solid brick walls</h3>
        <p>Victorian houses have solid brick walls, not the cavity walls of later homes. It is hard to bury pipes that run across a solid wall. So we either run them under the floor, or fix them to the wall inside neat boxing. Both work well. Both take more planning and time than a modern house.</p>

        <h3>Suspended timber floors</h3>
        <p>Most Victorian terraces have wooden floors on the ground floor, with a gap below. This helps. We can run pipes under the boards, between the joists, without spoiling your floors. The catch is access. Some gaps are too shallow, or the joists run the wrong way. We check this at the free survey and plan the best route.</p>

        <h3>High ceilings and large rooms</h3>
        <p>Victorian rooms are often 3 metres high or more, and larger than modern rooms. So each room needs more heat. Radiators must be sized to match. A radiator that is too small will never heat a high room properly. We work out the heat each room needs at the free survey.</p>

        <h3>Original chimney flues</h3>
        <p>Many Victorian terraces still have their old chimney breasts. Any old flue must be checked before a gas appliance uses it. A damaged flue liner must be replaced before it is safe to use.</p>

        <h3>Lead pipework</h3>
        <p>Some Victorian houses still have old lead pipes, often on the cold water supply. They may still work. But we advise you to replace them during any big heating job. The water rules do not allow new lead pipes for drinking water. We point out any lead pipes we find at the survey.</p>

        <h2>How Much Does Central Heating Cost in a Liverpool Victorian Terrace?</h2>
        <p>A full central heating system in a 3-bed Victorian terrace starts from £5,295 inc VAT. A 2-bed starts from £4,495. The final price depends on the size of your home, how easy the pipe routes are, and the boiler you choose. You get a fixed written price after a free survey.</p>

        <table class="price-table brx-table stk stk-price lbl3">
          <thead><tr><th>Property Type</th><th>Typical Cost</th><th>Radiators</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>2-bed Victorian terrace</td><td class="price-td">From £4,495</td><td data-label="Radiators">Up to 6</td></tr>
            <tr><td>3-bed Victorian terrace</td><td class="price-td">From £5,295</td><td data-label="Radiators">7 to 10</td></tr>
            <tr><td>4-bed Victorian semi or end terrace</td><td class="price-td">From £6,295</td><td data-label="Radiators">11 to 13</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Large Victorian property or conversion</td><td class="price-td">Priced at free survey</td><td data-label="Radiators">14 or more</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <p>All prices include VAT.</p>

        <h2>Radiator Choices for Victorian Properties</h2>
        <p>Standard double panel radiators give more heat for the money than column radiators. They work well in period homes. If you want a period look, column radiators suit Victorian rooms. They come in two or four columns, and up to 1800mm tall for high rooms.</p>
        <p>Many Victorian terraces have bay windows. A radiator in the bay helps stop cold air from the big window. But it must be sized for the whole room, not just the bay. A radiator that is too small in the bay is a common cause of a cold front room.</p>

        <h2>Combi vs System Boiler in a Victorian Terrace</h2>
        <p>A combi boiler suits most 2 and 3-bed terraces with one bathroom. It heats water when you need it, so you do not need a cylinder. It is also compact. A system boiler with a hot water cylinder is better for bigger houses with more than one bathroom. We will tell you which is right for your home at the free survey.</p>

        <h2>Period Feature Preservation</h2>
        <p>We plan every pipe route to protect original features where we can. We do not cut through coving or damage fine plasterwork or old skirting boards without need. We talk you through the options before we start. Together we agree the neatest route. Where pipes must go on the wall, we use neat boxing to hide them.</p>

