        <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
        <div class="pf-faq">
          <details class="pf-faq-item"><summary><span class="pf-faq-tag pf-tag-green">COST</span><span class="pf-faq-q">How much does central heating cost in a Liverpool Victorian terrace?</span></summary><div class="pf-faq-body"><p>A full system in a 3-bed Victorian terrace starts from £5,295 inc VAT. A 2-bed starts from £4,495 and a 4-bed from £6,295. The price depends on size, pipe routes and the boiler you choose. You get a fixed price after a free survey.</p></div></details>
          <details class="pf-faq-item"><summary><span class="pf-faq-tag pf-tag-navy">PIPE</span><span class="pf-faq-q">How do you run pipes in a solid-walled Victorian property?</span></summary><div class="pf-faq-body"><p>Where we can, we run pipes under the wooden floors. That means no pipes on show and no damage to your walls. Where floors are solid or hard to reach, we fix pipes to the wall in neat boxing. We find the best route at the free survey.</p></div></details>
          <details class="pf-faq-item"><summary><span class="pf-faq-tag pf-tag-orange">RADIATOR</span><span class="pf-faq-q">Should I use column radiators in a Victorian property?</span></summary><div class="pf-faq-body"><p>Column radiators suit Victorian rooms well. They come in two or four columns, and up to 1800mm tall for high rooms. Standard double panel radiators also work well and give more heat for the money. We help you choose the style and size at the free survey.</p></div></details>
          <details class="pf-faq-item"><summary><span class="pf-faq-tag pf-tag-navy">BOILER</span><span class="pf-faq-q">Is a combi boiler suitable for a Liverpool Victorian terrace?</span></summary><div class="pf-faq-body"><p>A combi suits most 2 and 3-bed terraces with one bathroom. Bigger houses with more than one bathroom do better with a system boiler and a hot water cylinder. That way, more than one tap can run hot at once. We advise on the right type at the free survey.</p></div></details>
          <details class="pf-faq-item"><summary><span class="pf-faq-tag pf-tag-orange">LEAD</span><span class="pf-faq-q">What if my Victorian property still has lead pipework?</span></summary><div class="pf-faq-body"><p>We point out any lead pipes at the survey. They may still work. But the water rules do not allow new lead pipes for drinking water, and we advise you to replace old ones. We can include this in your quote.</p></div></details>
        </div>
      </div>

